  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 141 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHigh pressure aloft across the Desert Southwest will gradually \nweaken as deep troughing dives southeast from western Canada. \nSoutherly flow will support warmer temperatures with a return to \nupper 80s to lower 90s for Thursday and Friday. Broad upper-level \nheight falls from the approaching longwave trough will overspread \nthe area. Low pressure across the Northern Plains will drag a cold \nfront south into the area during the afternoon/evening hours. NBM \nQPF probabilities continue to favor areas to our south and east and \nas a result, the going forecast keep most of the forecast area dry. \nThat said, deterministic solutions vary slightly on placement \nof this boundary by roughly 100-200 miles. Any slower \nprogression with this feature will bode more favorable for local\nrain chances thus confidence in precipitation potential remains\nlow for now. More unsettled weather will be on tap for the \nweekend as enhanced mid- level flow resides immediately north of\nthe area. Though upper-level dynamics will largely miss the \nimmediate area, reasonable moisture fetch from the Gulf combined\nwith mid-level moisture originating from the Eastern Pacific \nwill combine to produce PWAT values nearly 1-2 sigma above \nnormal values for mid-September. NBM remains fairly noisy with \nbroad low-probabilities for wetting rain and this closely aligns\nwith the latest EPS solution. That said, deterministic \nECMWF/GFS solutions are fairly aggressive with rainfall over the\nweekend but particularly Sunday night as a surface low takes \nshape in the lee of the Central Rockies. This will be something \nto watch in the coming days including a potential severe threat \nwith stronger mid-level flow supporting a sheared environment. \nOf course the question remains where the frontal boundary sets \nup. NBM inner- quartile spreads remain large, 20F for North \nPlatte on Sunday, highlighting this uncertainty.\n\nTowards the middle of next week, upper-level ridging will redevelop \nover the southern CONUS with increasing influence across Nebraska. \nThough NBM MaxT spread remains large, gradual day over day increases \nare noted from Monday through Thursday. While heights aloft will \nincrease, non-zero precipitation potential will exist. Though no one \nday appears to be a washout, recurring rain chances should be enough \nto keep us on the wetter side of normal for the time frame. This \naligns with the latest Climate Prediction Center 8-14 outlook which \nhedges towards > 40% probabilities of above normal precipitation for \nTuesday through the end of next week. Details are far from certain \nso continue to check back with later forecasts for details.",
